::: {.doc-subject}

Remove duplicate edges, keeping only one edge per node pair.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L3618-L3758){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.remove_duplicate_edges(keep_distinct_attributes=False)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Remove duplicate edges](../assets/graphs/remove-duplicate-edges.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Before: a four-node cycle with two parallel edges between C and D, the second in the accent color, and a self-loop on B. After: only one C to D edge remains and the duplicate is shown dashed, while the self-loop is left in place." width="100%"}

For each pair of nodes with multiple edges between them, keeps only the first edge (by edge
ID order) and removes all others. This converts a multigraph into a simple graph with
respect to multi-edges, but does not remove self-loops.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[keep_distinct_attributes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `False` (the default), edges are considered duplicates based solely on their source
    and target nodes, regardless of weight, `rel`, or other attributes. If `True`, edges
    with different attribute values (weight, `rel`, or custom attributes) are considered
    distinct and will not be removed.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

By default the notion of "duplicate" ignores attributes entirely: two edges $(u, v)$
with different weights or `rel` values still count as the same edge, and only the
first one (the one with the lowest edge ID) survives. Flip `keep_distinct_attributes=True`
to switch to attribute-aware deduplication, in which case only edges that agree on
*every* attribute collapse together. Directedness matters: in a directed graph $(u, v)$
and $(v, u)$ are treated as separate pairs and can coexist, while in an undirected
graph they are the same edge. Self-loops are untouched, so reach for `remove_selfloops()`
or `simplify()` for those. To survey the damage first, `which_multiple()` lists the
duplicate edge IDs and `count_multiple()` totals duplicates per node pair.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~which_multiple`) 

:   The IDs of the parallel edges.

[](`~remove_selfloops`) 

:   Remove self-loops.

[](`~simplify`) 

:   Remove both self-loops and parallel edges.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

By default, edges are considered duplicates purely by their endpoints, regardless of
weight, so these three parallel "A"-"B" edges collapse down to one, the first (lowest
edge ID):

::: {#45c406a6 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

g = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B", 1.0), ("A", "B", 2.0), ("A", "B", 3.0)]
)
g.remove_duplicate_edges()

g.count_edges(), g.get_edge_df()["weight"].to_list()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(1, [1.0])
```
:::
:::


Setting `keep_distinct_attributes=True` treats edges with different attribute values
as genuinely different, so only the true duplicate (the second "friend" edge) is
removed:

::: {#761cc50f .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
g = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [
        ("A", "B", {"rel": "friend"}),
        ("A", "B", {"rel": "colleague"}),
        ("A", "B", {"rel": "friend"}),  # true duplicate of the first edge
    ]
).remove_duplicate_edges(keep_distinct_attributes=True)

g.get_edge_df()["rel"].to_list()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
['friend', 'colleague']
```
:::
:::


### Payments exported twice

A bank's export can list a payment twice, here Acme's wages to Eve and one of Dee's
coffees. Dee also bought a second coffee on another day, which is a real payment:

::: {#9bb35a62 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
export = [
    ("Acme", "Dee", 2600, {"date": "2026-03-01"}),
    ("Acme", "Eve", 2700, {"date": "2026-03-01"}),
    ("Acme", "Eve", 2700, {"date": "2026-03-01"}),
    ("Dee", "Grocer", 64, {"date": "2026-03-03"}),
    ("Dee", "Cafe", 12, {"date": "2026-03-04"}),
    ("Dee", "Cafe", 12, {"date": "2026-03-04"}),
    ("Dee", "Cafe", 12, {"date": "2026-03-09"}),
    ("Eve", "Grocer", 38, {"date": "2026-03-04"}),
]


def load():
    return ec.Graph.from_edges(export, directed=True)


load().which_multiple()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
[2, 3, 5, 6, 7]
```
:::
:::


By endpoints alone, all three of Dee's coffees are one payment. With
`keep_distinct_attributes=True`, only rows that agree on the amount and the date as well
collapse, so the two coffees are kept and the exported-twice rows go:

::: {#1563875f .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
distinct = load().remove_duplicate_edges(keep_distinct_attributes=True)

load().remove_duplicate_edges().count_edges(), distinct.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}
```
(5, 6)
```
:::
:::


Before, the parallel edges are drawn as curves. After, the dates show the two coffees
that were kept:

::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#88f1d8f0 .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
load().plot(label_by="label", seed=1, width=360, height=360)
```

